      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people in a circle:**\n\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circle is \\((n-1)!\\). Therefore, the total number of ways to arrange 9 people in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Treat the block of 4 math majors as a single \"super person\":**\n\n   Since the 4 math majors must sit together, we can consider them as a single unit or \"super person\". This reduces the problem to arranging 6 \"people\" (the block of 4 math majors and the other 5 individuals) in a circle. The number of ways to arrange 6 people in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the number of favorable arrangements:**\n\n   Within the block of 4 math majors, the 4 math majors can be arranged among themselves in:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n   ways. Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 6 \"people\" in a circle and the number of ways to arrange the 4 math majors within their block:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability:**\n\n   The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the ratio of the number of favorable arrangements to the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\\).",
