      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we need to consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to seat the nine people around a round table.**\n   Since the table is round, we fix one person in one seat to avoid counting rotations as different arrangements. The remaining 8 people can be arranged in \\(8!\\) (8 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively.**\n   Treat the four math majors as a single \"block\". This block can be arranged among the 4 math majors in \\(4!\\) (4 factorial) ways.\n   Within this block, the four math majors can be arranged among themselves in \\(4!\\) ways.\n   After arranging the block, we have 6 \"slots\" to place the remaining 5 people (3 physics majors and 2 biology majors), and we need to ensure that the block (math majors) is in one of these slots. There are 6 possible slots for the block.\n   Thus, the number of favorable arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 4! \\times 4! = 6 \\times 24 \\times 24 = 3456\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3456}{40320} = \\frac{3456 \\div 576}{40320 \\div 576} = \\frac{6}{70} = \\frac{3}{35}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{35}}\\).",
